## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a plug-in style, runtime-extensible CLI using classic design patterns with a minimal surface area.

## Key Concepts

- **Registry Pattern**: Maps command names to factories for dynamic lookup
- **Factory Pattern**: Creates command instances on demand
- **Dispatcher**: Resolves and executes commands with provided arguments
- **Conflict Handling**: Strategy-based approach (overwrite, warn, or reject)
- **Help Generation**: Aggregates help() output from all registered commands

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Command {
  execute(args: string[]): Promise<any>;
  help(): string;
}

type CommandFactory = () => Command;

class CommandRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory, allowOverwrite = false): void {
    if (this.factories.has(name) && !allowOverwrite) {
      throw new Error(`Command '${name}' already registered`);
    }
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  getFactory(name: string): CommandFactory | undefined {
    return this.factories.get(name);
  }

  getAllCommands(): string[] {
    return Array.from(this.factories.keys());
  }
}

class Dispatcher {
  constructor(private registry: CommandRegistry) {}

  async dispatch(name: string, args: string[]): Promise<any> {
    const factory = this.registry.getFactory(name);
    if (!factory) {
      throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    }
    return factory().execute(args);
  }

  help(): string {
    const commands = this.registry.getAllCommands();
    return commands.map(name => {
      const factory = this.registry.getFactory(name)!;
      return factory().help();
    }).join('\n');
  }
}

// Usage:
const registry = new CommandRegistry();
const dispatcher = new Dispatcher(registry);

registry.register('build', () => ({ execute: async () => { /*...*/ }, help: () => 'build: Build the project' }));

await dispatcher.dispatch('build', ['--prod']);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores runtime extensibility using the Decorator and Registry patterns in a practical CLI context.

## Key Concepts
- **Registry pattern**: Centralized storage for cross-cutting concerns
- **Decorator pattern**: Wraps commands in layers for logging, retry, and metrics
- **Per-command isolation**: Faults in one decorator don't cascade to others
- **Runtime composition**: Decorator chains are assembled at execution time, not compile time

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  execute(ctx: any): Promise<void>;
}

interface Decorator extends Command {
  setNext(cmd: Command): Command;
}

class DecoratorRegistry {
  private decorators: Decorator[] = [];

  register(decorator: Decorator) {
    this.decorators.push(decorator);
  }

  compose(cmd: Command): Command {
    return this.decorators.reduceRight(
      (inner, decorator) => decorator.setNext(inner),
      cmd
    );
  }
}

const registry = new DecoratorRegistry();

registry.register({
  setNext(cmd) {
    return {
      execute: async (ctx) => {
        console.log('Logging:', ctx);
        await cmd.execute(ctx);
      },
    };
  },
} as Decorator);

class CommandFactory {
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>();

  register(name: string, cmd: Command) {
    this.commands.set(name, registry.compose(cmd));
  }

  execute(name: string, ctx: any) {
    return this.commands.get(name)!.execute(ctx);
  }
}

const factory = new CommandFactory();
factory.register('deploy', {
  execute: async (ctx) => console.log('Deploying:', ctx),
});

factory.execute('deploy', { env: 'prod' });
```

## Key Takeaway
Adding a new cross-cutting concern requires only registering a new decorator—no modifications to existing commands or the factory.

